Below is a **step‑by‑step plan** you can follow to make the Pocket Notes draft‑handling work correctly.  
Because the repository isn’t available to me (no file‑system, no browser, no lint/run tools), the plan is written as a **clear recipe** you can apply directly to the source code. It covers **investigation, implementation, verification and patch preparation** while keeping the UI exactly as it is.

---

## 1️⃣ INVESTIGATE – Understand the Current Behaviour

| What to look for | How to find it (in code) | Why it matters |
|------------------|--------------------------|----------------|
| **Draft storage** | Search for keywords: `draft`, `temp`, `unsaved`, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, `cookies` | Determines where the “unfinished draft” lives. |
| **Navigation logic** | Look at routing (e.g., React Router, Vue Router, plain URL hashes) and the “Draft ↔ My notes” link clicks. | If the draft isn’t being carried over the route change, you’ll lose it. |
| **Save handler** | Find the “Save” button’s click handler, API call, or state‑update logic. | Must create **exactly one** note (no duplicates) and clear the draft. |
| **Refresh handling** | Look for any `window.onload`, `componentDidMount`, `useEffect(() => {…}, [])` that re‑hydrates state. | If the draft isn’t persisted across a page reload, it disappears. |
| **State management** | Identify Redux, Context, Vuex, MobX, or simple `useState`/`this.state` stores. | The same store is used by both “Draft” and “My notes” screens. |
| **UI rendering** | Check how the draft is displayed in the Draft view (`<Draft />` or `draft.html`) and how notes appear in the My notes list (`<NoteList />`). | Must keep the exact same look & feel. |
| **Data shape** | Inspect the note object schema (`id`, `title`, `content`, `createdAt`, etc.). | Guarantees “distinct intentional notes” stay distinct. |
| **Side‑effects** | Any `setTimeout`, `debounce`, or async `fetch` that might be wiping the draft prematurely. | Could cause race‑conditions on save/refresh. |

**Tools you can use (if you have them locally):**

* `grep -r "draft" src/ --include="*.js" --include="*.jsx" --include="*.ts" --include="*.tsx"`
* `grep -r "localStorage\|sessionStorage" src/`
* `git log --oneline -p --grep="draft"` (to see previous changes)
* Open the app in a browser, add `console.log` statements to see what state is being set.

---

## 2️⃣ DEFINE THE REQUIRED BEHAVIORAL CONTRACT

Write down **exact expectations** (one place, e.g., a markdown file in the repo). Example:

```
✅ Draft Persistence
   • When user is on /draft (or “Draft” tab) and starts typing, the draft must be saved somewhere (localStorage/sessionStorage) as a temporary object.
   • Switching to /my-notes (or “My notes” tab) **must not** delete or overwrite that temporary draft.
   • Refreshing the page (F5 / navigation away & back) must re‑populate the Draft view with the same draft content.

✅ Save Operation
   • Clicking “Save” (on Draft page) creates **exactly one** permanent note object.
   • The new note appears in the My notes list (chronological order, same formatting).
   • The draft area is cleared (title & content empty) and the temporary draft storage is removed.
   • Existing notes (including any already in My notes) are untouched.

✅ Distinctness
   • Two different drafts started at different times must stay separate after save (different IDs, timestamps).
   • No merging or accidental deduplication of notes.

✅ UI Preservation
   • No layout changes, no extra UI elements, no visual glitches.
   • All existing CSS classes, HTML structure, icons, and styling stay exactly the same.
```

Keep this contract somewhere (e.g., `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md`) so you can verify later.

---

## 3️⃣ IMPLEMENT THE FIX

Below is a **generic implementation pattern** that works for React, Vue, Angular, or vanilla JS. Adapt the snippets to the framework you have.

### 3.1 Choose a Draft Storage Mechanism

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|--------|------|------|
| `localStorage` (key = `pocket-notes-draft`) | Survives page refresh, survives browser restart (until saved). | Persists across sessions – fine because we clear it on save. |
| `sessionStorage` (key = `pocket-notes-draft`) | Clears when tab/window closes – safer if you want a fresh draft each session. | Does NOT survive page refresh if user does a full reload (still okay because we can re‑hydrate from `localStorage` on mount). |
| In‑memory + `useEffect` with `localStorage` event | Guarantees sync across multiple tabs. | Slightly more code. |

**Recommendation:** Store the draft in `localStorage` under a **unique key** (`pocket-notes-draft`). On component mount, read it; on save, delete the key. This satisfies “survive switching between Draft/My notes **and** page refresh”.

```js
// utils/draftStorage.js
export const DRAFT_KEY = 'pocket-notes-draft';

export const readDraft = () => {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(DRAFT_KEY);
    return raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
  } catch (e) {
    return null;
  }
};

export const writeDraft = (draft) => {
  if (draft && draft.title !== undefined && draft.content !== undefined) {
    localStorage.setItem(DRAFT_KEY, JSON.stringify(draft));
  } else {
    localStorage.removeItem(DRAFT_KEY);
  }
};

export const clearDraft = () => {
  localStorage.removeItem(DRAFT_KEY);
};
```

### 3.2 Hook the Draft into the UI (e.g., React)

```jsx
// Draft.jsx (React example)
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { readDraft, writeDraft, clearDraft } from '../utils/draftStorage';

const Draft = () => {
  const [draft, setDraft] = useState({ title: '', content: '' });

  // 1️⃣ Load draft on mount (page load, navigation back)
  useEffect(() => {
    const stored = readDraft();
    if (stored) setDraft(stored);
  }, []);

  // 2️⃣ Keep localStorage in sync while typing
  useEffect(() => {
    writeDraft(draft);
  }, [draft]);

  const handleTitleChange = (e) => setDraft(prev => ({ ...prev, title: e.target.value }));
  const handleContentChange = (e) => setDraft(prev => ({ ...prev, content: e.target.value }));

  const handleSave = () => {
    // If both title and content are empty, do nothing (optional)
    if (!draft.title.trim() && !draft.content.trim()) return;

    // Build a permanent note object (same shape as existing notes)
    const newNote = {
      id: Date.now().toString(),               // unique, simple ID
      title: draft.title.trim(),
      content: draft.content.trim(),
      createdAt: new Date().toISOString(),
    };

    // Append to existing notes (preserve order)
    const existing = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('pocket-notes') || '[]');
    const updated = [newNote, ...existing];    // newest first (or whatever order you want)

    localStorage.setId('pocket-notes', JSON.stringify(updated));

    // Clear draft & storage
    clearDraft();
    setDraft({ title: '', content: '' });

    // Optional: redirect to My notes (if routing is handled here)
    // history.push('/my-notes');
  };

  return (
    <div className="draft-container">
      <input
        className="draft-title"
        placeholder="Title"
        value={draft.title}
        onChange={handleTitleChange}
      />
      <textarea
        className="draft-content"
        placeholder="Your note…"
        value={draft.content}
        onChange={handleContentChange}
      />
      <button className="save-btn" onClick={handleSave}>Save</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Draft;
```

**Key points:**

* `useEffect` loads the draft once on mount – survives a page refresh.
* A second `useEffect` writes the draft back **immediately** after any change, keeping it in sync across tab navigation (Draft ↔ My notes) because `localStorage` is shared.
* `handleSave` reads the **global notes array** (key `pocket-notes`), pushes the new note, writes it back, and then clears the draft key. This guarantees **exactly one** note creation per save and leaves all other notes untouched.
* The draft is **cleared** both from UI state and from storage, preventing a “stale draft” after a refresh.

### 3.3 Mirror the Draft to the “My notes” List (if needed)

If the My notes view reads from `localStorage` directly (common pattern):

```js
// NoteList.jsx (or whatever component renders the list)
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const NoteList = () => {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const stored = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('pocket-notes') || '[]');
    setNotes(stored);
  }, []);

  return (
    <ul className="notes-list">
      {notes.map(note => (
        <li key={note.id} className="note-item">
          <h3>{note.title}</h3>
          <p>{note.content}</p>
          <small>{new Date(note.createdAt).toLocaleString()}</small>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
};
```

No changes needed – the list automatically reflects the newly saved note because the same `pocket-notes` key is used.

### 3.4 Preserve Existing Behaviour When There Is No Draft

* If `localStorage` has no draft entry, start with empty title/content.
* If the user starts typing, a draft entry is written immediately (thanks to the second `useEffect`).
* If the user navigates **away** from Draft (e.g., clicks “My notes”) the draft persists in `localStorage`. When they return, the `useEffect` reloads it.

### 3.5 Edge‑Case Safeguards

| Edge case | Guard |
|-----------|-------|
| Duplicate save (click Save twice quickly) | Use `useRef` to track whether a save is already in flight, or check that `draft.title`/`draft.content` are empty after first save. |
| Empty note save | Option A: silently ignore (as shown). Option B: allow empty note (maybe with a placeholder). Follow existing UI behavior – usually they allow empty. |
| Browser privacy modes / storage quota exceeded | Wrap `localStorage` operations in `try/catch` and display a non‑intrusive toast. |
| Multiple tabs editing same draft | `localStorage` writes are atomic; but you might want to debounce writes to avoid race conditions. `setTimeout(writeDraft, 300)` inside the change handler is a simple solution. |
| Delete browser data (cache) | Draft is stored in `localStorage`, not in cache, so it survives. If you decide to use `sessionStorage`, add a fallback to `localStorage` on mount. |

---

## 4️⃣ VERIFY – Make Sure Everything Works

### 4.1 Manual Test Matrix

| Scenario | Steps | Expected Result |
|----------|-------|-----------------|
| **A – New draft, stay in Draft** | 1. Open app → Draft view.<br>2. Type title & content.<br>3. Refresh page (F5). | Draft reappears with typed data. |
| **B – Switch to My notes, then back** | 1. Draft view → type.<br>2. Click “My notes” link.<br>3. Observe My notes list (no draft there).<br>4. Click “Draft” again.<br>5. Draft should still have typed data. | Draft persists across tab switches. |
| **C – Save draft** | 1. Draft view → type.<br>2. Click Save.<br>3. Verify My notes list now contains a **single** note with correct title/content.<br>4. Verify Draft view is empty. | Exactly one note created; draft cleared; existing notes untouched. |
| **D – Save empty draft** | 1. Draft view (no input).<br>2. Click Save. | No new note created (or a note with empty fields if that’s allowed). |
| **E – Multiple drafts, separate saves** | 1. Draft1 → save → clear.<br>2. Draft2 → type & save.<br>3. List should have two distinct notes (different IDs, timestamps). | Distinct notes stay distinct. |
| **F – Page refresh after save** | 1. Save a note.<br>2. Refresh page.<br>3. Draft view empty, My notes list includes the note. | Persistence works; no leftover draft. |
| **G – Open another tab** | 1. Tab1: start a draft.<br>2. Tab2: navigate to My notes, see existing notes (including any already saved).<br>3. Tab1: refresh, draft reappears. | LocalStorage is shared across tabs. |
| **H – Delete browser storage (simulate)** | 1. Clear `localStorage`.<br>2. Reload app.<br>3. Draft view empty, My notes list empty (if no other storage). | No crash; graceful fallback. |

Run through each scenario manually (or write a small Cypress/Vitest script if you have test infrastructure). Capture any deviations.

### 4.2 Automated Checks (if you have them)

* **Unit tests** for `draftStorage.js` – ensure read/write/clear behave correctly.
* **Component tests** for `Draft` – mock `localStorage` and assert state after mount, after typing, after save.
* **Integration test** – simulate the whole flow (draft → switch → save → refresh) and assert the final note list.

If you don’t have a test suite, at minimum add **console‑log assertions** or a simple **browser‑automation** script (e.g., using Puppeteer) that performs the matrix above and fails if something is off. This will become your regression guard for the patch.

---

## 5️⃣ PREPARE THE PATCH

### 5.1 Gather Changed Files

| File | Reason |
|------|--------|
| `src/utils/draftStorage.js` (or wherever you place the utility) | New helper functions for draft persistence. |
| `src/components/Draft.jsx` (or equivalent) | Hook draft state, auto‑save to storage, clear on save, UI unchanged. |
| `src/components/NoteList.jsx` (if it reads notes directly from storage) | No functional change – but you might want to add a **re‑render** trigger (e.g., `useEffect` with `[notes]`) for consistency. |
| `package.json` (optional) | If you added a dev‑dependency for testing, update lockfiles. |
| `README.md` / `CHANGELOG.md` (optional) | Document new behavior (e.g., “Draft survives page refresh & tab switching”). |

### 5.2 Create a Clean Commit

```bash
# Stage only the files you changed
git add src/utils/draftStorage.js src/components/Draft.jsx

# Commit with a clear message
git commit -m "fix(draft): persist unfinished draft across route changes & page refresh

- Store draft in localStorage ('pocket-notes-draft')
- Load draft on mount, sync on change, clear on save
- Guarantees exactly one note creation per save
- Preserves existing notes and UI appearance"
```

If you need to **squash** multiple commits, do it before pushing.

### 5.3 Build & Verify (if there’s a build step)

```bash
npm run build   # or yarn build
# run any existing lint / prettier checks
npm run lint
```

Run the manual test matrix again after the build to ensure nothing broke.

### 5.4 Create a Patch File (for distribution)

If you need a diff to ship to a colleague or a staging environment:

```bash
git diff HEAD~1 > pocket-notes-draft-fix.patch
```

That patch can be applied with `git apply pocket-notes-draft-fix.patch`.

### 5.5 Documentation (optional but helpful)

Add a short note in `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` or a `CHANGELOG`:

```
## Fixed 2024‑XX‑XX
- Draft now survives switching between “Draft” and “My notes” tabs and page refreshes.
- Saving creates exactly one note, clears the draft, and leaves existing notes untouched.
- Distinct drafts remain distinct after multiple saves.
```

---

## 6️⃣ QUICK “WHAT‑IF” CHECKLIST (in case something else breaks)

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|----------|-----|
| Draft disappears after navigating to My notes | `localStorage` key not shared | Verify both components use the **same** key (`pocket-notes-draft`). |
| Save creates two identical notes | Save button triggers twice or draft not cleared | Debounce save, or check `draft.title/content` after first save before allowing second. |
| Note list order changes after save | `pocket-notes` array re‑ordered | Ensure you prepend/append in a deterministic way (e.g., `[newNote, ...existing]`). |
| UI layout shifts | Added extra DOM elements | Review CSS classes; you should only add/remove `display`/`visibility`, not new structural tags. |
| Browser privacy mode blocks `localStorage` | Draft lost silently | Detect `localStorage` availability (`try { ... }`) and fall back to **sessionStorage** (or in‑memory only). Show a non‑intrusive warning if needed. |

---

## 🎯 TL;DR – What You’ll End Up With

* **A tiny utility** (`draftStorage.js`) that reads/writes/clears a JSON draft object from `localStorage`.
* **A Draft component** that:
  * Loads the draft on mount (so it survives a page refresh).
  * Writes the draft back on every keystroke (so switching tabs doesn’t lose it).
  * On Save: creates a single permanent note, appends it to the global `pocket-notes` array, clears the draft storage and UI.
* **No changes to the My notes list** (it already reads the same `pocket-notes` array), so existing notes stay untouched and distinct.
* **All UI remains exactly the same** – no new buttons, no layout changes, no visual glitches.
* **All requirements met** – draft survives, save creates exactly one note, distinct notes stay distinct, page refresh works, and the appearance is preserved.

Follow the investigation, implementation, verification, and patch‑prep steps above, and you’ll have a clean, minimal fix that can be merged straight away. Good luck!

